<p id="mwBA"><i id="mwBQ"><b id="mwBg">Mycobacterium pinnipedii</b></i> is a member of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Mycobacterium_tuberculosis_complex" title="Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex" id="mwBw"><i id="mwCA">Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i> complex</a> which primarily infects seals.  It is a slowly growing pathogenic <i id="mwCQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Mycobacterium" title="Mycobacterium" id="mwCg">Mycobacterium</a></i>.  The species was first isolated from the lungs of wild seals in Australia by Debra Cousins and colleagues and was initially called the 'seal bacillus'<sup about="#mwt6" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-1"}}'><a href="./Mycobacterium_pinnipedii#cite_note-1" id="mwCw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQ">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDg">]</span></span></a></sup>.</p>

<p id="mwDw">The proposal for identification of the seal bacillus as a new species within the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Mycobacterium_tuberculosis_complex" title="Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex" id="mwEA"><i id="mwEQ">Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i> complex</a> was presented for the first time by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Niyaz_Ahmed" title="Niyaz Ahmed" id="mwEg">Niyaz Ahmed</a> and colleagues based on comprehensive comparative genomic analyses using fluorescent amplified fragment length polymorphisms <sup about="#mwt9" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-2"}}'><a href="./Mycobacterium_pinnipedii#cite_note-2" id="mwEw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFQ">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFg">]</span></span></a></sup>. Later, the species was taxonomically and validly named by Cousins et al. in 2003 as <i id="mwFw"><b id="mwGA">Mycobacterium pinnipedii</b></i> after the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Pinnipeds" title="Pinnipeds" class="mw-redirect" id="mwGQ">pinniped</a> species which served as primary hosts of the pathogen<sup about="#mwt12" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="./Mycobacterium_pinnipedii#cite_note-3" id="mwGg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwGw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHA">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHQ">]</span></span></a></sup>.</p>

<p id="mwHg">In 2014, a genetic study showed that a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Peruvian" title="Peruvian" class="mw-redirect" id="mwHw">Peruvian</a> human skeleton dating to 1000 CE had been infected with a form of tuberculosis most closely related to <i id="mwIA">M. pinnipedii</i>, suggesting that seals had served as a vector for transmission of tuberculosis from the Old World to the New.<sup about="#mwt15" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Bos_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Bos"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Bos-4"}}'><a href="./Mycobacterium_pinnipedii#cite_note-Bos-4" id="mwIQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwIg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

